% bool2int, satisfiable: the seam between the Boolean and the integer half, with BOTH
% directions of the channelling load-bearing in the printed answer.
%
% lib/core/prop/bool2int.ml documents four pushes -- lo and hi, each way -- and warns
% that a channelling constraint which prunes one way only is the classic silent
% weakness. This model is built so that each direction does something the other cannot
% do for it:
%
%   b -> x, on the upper bound. `p' and `q' are declared `var bool', so their domains
%     are [0, 1] (D-0007). Channelling confines x and y to [0, 1] as well, DOWN FROM
%     their declared 0..5. Without that push the linear row `x + y >= 2' is satisfied
%     by x = 0, y = 2 and nothing forces either Boolean true -- the printed answer
%     would be p = false, q = false, x = 0, y = 2.
%
%   x -> b, on the lower bound. With x, y <= 1 in hand the linear row makes x >= 1 and
%     y >= 1, and it is the x -> b direction that turns those integer bounds into
%     p = true and q = true. This is the direction whose absence a model alone has
%     trouble seeing -- the search would recover the same answer by branching -- so it
%     is pinned as an assertion rather than as a sentence here:
%     test/unit/test_prop.ml's "bool2int: x -> b" checks push it directly.
%
% Ground truth, with no reference to the search: bool2int makes x = p and y = q, both
% in {0, 1}; x + y >= 2 then forces x = y = 1, hence p = q = true. Unique solution.
% Both Booleans print TRUE and both integers print 1, all four the opposite of what a
% solver that ignored `bool2int' would print.
var bool: p :: output_var;
var bool: q :: output_var;
var 0..5: x :: output_var;
var 0..5: y :: output_var;
constraint bool2int(p, x);
constraint bool2int(q, y);
constraint int_lin_le([-1, -1], [x, y], -2);
solve satisfy;
